<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glorious Revolution (Spain)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Spanish Revolution of 1868, La Gloriosa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Glorious Revolution of September 1868 was a military rising led by Generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-prim">Juan Prim</a> and Francisco Serrano and Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-topete">Juan Bautista Topete</a> that drove Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-ii-of-spain">Isabella II of Spain</a> into exile. It opened the Sexenio Democrático, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a>, the liberal Constitution of 1869 and the election of Amadeo I as king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain.jpg" alt="Glorious Revolution (Spain)" width="314" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 19th century Spanish engraver/printer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alegoria_Revolucionaria_La_Gloriosa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the 1860s the reign of Queen Isabella II had become deeply unpopular. Her government was plagued by corruption, economic crises and political repression. She had alienated liberals and progressives and become more dependent on conservative and clerical factions, and her troubled marriage and other scandals further eroded public confidence. Earlier attempts to overthrow her had failed, most notably in 1854 and 1861. An 1866 rebellion led by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-prim">Juan Prim</a> and a revolt of the sergeants at the San Gil barracks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> showed liberals and republicans that there was serious unrest to be harnessed.</p><p>Exiles made agreements at Ostend in 1866 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1867. The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldo-odonnell">Leopoldo O'Donnell</a> in 1867 caused the Unión Liberal to unravel, and many of its supporters joined the movement against Isabella.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In September 1868 naval forces under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-topete">Juan Bautista Topete</a> mutinied in Cádiz, the city where Rafael del Riego had launched his coup against Isabella's father, Ferdinand VII, half a century earlier. When Generals Prim and Francisco Serrano denounced the government, much of the army defected to them as they arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The queen made a brief show of force at the Battle of Alcolea, where her loyal generals under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-pavia-y-rodriguez-de-alburquerque">Manuel Pavía</a> were defeated by Serrano. Isabella crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and retired from Spanish politics. She lived in exile at the Palacio Castilla in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> until her death in 1904.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The coalition of liberals, moderates and republicans lacked direction. Control passed to Serrano, who was named regent while a monarch was sought. In 1869 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> promulgated a liberal constitution, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> since 1812, and Prim was also named regent that year. Candidates for the throne included the elderly Espartero, who rejected the idea but still received eight votes, Isabella's son Alfonso and Ferdinand of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>. Politicians feared that offering the crown to Prince Leopold of Hohenzollern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a> would trigger a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. In August 1870 the Cortes chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeo-i-of-spain">Amadeo of Savoy</a>, a younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, who was elected King on 3 November 1870.</p><p>Amadeo landed at Cartagena on 27 December, the day Prim was assassinated as he left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a>. He reigned two years, after which the parties formed the first Spanish Republic, which also lasted two years. In 1875 the Cortes proclaimed Isabella's son King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Glorious Revolution (Spain)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Glorious Revolution</b> of 1868 overthrew Isabella II, the song had only a modest place in the First Spanish Republic (1873–1874), when &quot;La Marseillaise&quot; and the &quot;Himno de Garibaldi&quot; were more popular.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Glorious Revolution</b> of September 1868 drove her into exile, opening the Sexenio Democrático.</blockquote><a href="/state/bourbon-spain">Bourbon Spain</a></li><li><blockquote>In September 1868 a military rising led by Generals Juan Prim and Francisco Serrano and Admiral Juan Bautista Topete, known as the <b>Glorious Revolution</b>, drove Isabella II into exile.</blockquote><a href="/state/sexenio-democratico">Sexenio Democrático</a></li></ul></section>
